*/

DO $migration$
DECLARE
  v_topic_id uuid;
BEGIN

  /* ============================================================
     TOPIC 16: POLYMERS AND PLASTICS
     ============================================================ */
  SELECT id INTO v_topic_id FROM topics WHERE name ILIKE '%polymer%' OR name ILIKE '%plastic%' LIMIT 1;
  IF v_topic_id IS NOT NULL THEN
    DELETE FROM topic_sections WHERE topic_id = v_topic_id;
    INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
    (v_topic_id,
     'Introduction to Polymers: Monomers, Addition and Condensation',
     E'## Polymers: Giant Molecules\n\nA **polymer** is a large molecule (macromolecule) made up of many small repeating units called **monomers** joined together by covalent bonds. The process of joining monomers is called **polymerisation**.\n\n### Types of Polymerisation\n\n| Feature | Addition Polymerisation | Condensation Polymerisation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Monomers | Alkenes (C=C double bond) | Two different functional groups |\n| By-product | None | Small molecule (H2O, HCl) |\n| Bond formed | C-C single bond | Ester, amide, or other linkage |\n| Examples | Poly(ethene), PVC, PTFE | Nylon, Dacron, proteins, starch |\n\n### Addition Polymerisation\nIn addition polymerisation, the C=C double bond in alkene monomers opens up and the monomers link together in a chain. No atoms are lost.\n\n**Ethene to Poly(ethene):**\nnCH2=CH2 --> [-CH2-CH2-]n\n\n**Propene to Poly(propene):**\nnCH2=CHCH3 --> [-CH2-CH(CH3)-]n\n\n**Chloroethene (vinyl chloride) to PVC:**\nnCH2=CHCl --> [-CH2-CHCl-]n\n\n**Tetrafluoroethene to PTFE (Teflon):**\nnCF2=CF2 --> [-CF2-CF2-]n\n\n### How to Draw Repeating Units\n1. Remove the double bond from the monomer\n2. Add single bonds extending left and right\n3. Enclose in brackets with subscript n\n4. Each carbon gains one bond to the next monomer\n\n**Example: Styrene to Polystyrene**\n- Monomer: CH2=CH-C6H5 (phenylethene/styrene)\n- Repeating unit: [-CH2-CH(C6H5)-]n\n\n### Condensation Polymerisation\nIn condensation polymerisation, monomers have two reactive functional groups. Each time two monomers join, a small molecule (usually water) is released.\n\n**Nylon 6,6** (polyamide): Made from hexanedioic acid + 1,6-diaminohexane\n- Acid: HOOC-(CH2)4-COOH\n- Amine: H2N-(CH2)6-NH2\n- Linkage: -CO-NH- (amide bond)\n- By-product: H2O\n\n**Dacron/Terylene** (polyester): Made from benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid + ethane-1,2-diol\n- Linkage: -COO- (ester bond)\n- By-product: H2O\n\n**Biological Condensation Polymers:**\n- Proteins: amino acids joined by peptide (-CO-NH-) bonds\n- Starch/Cellulose: glucose joined by glycosidic bonds\n- DNA/RNA: nucleotides joined by phosphodiester bonds',
     'overview', 1),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Properties of Polymers: Thermoplastics, Thermosets and Natural Polymers',
     E'## Types of Synthetic Polymers\n\n### Thermoplastics\nThermoplastics soften on heating and can be reshaped. They have **linear or branched chains** with only weak van der Waals forces between chains.\n\n| Polymer | Monomer | Properties | Uses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Poly(ethene) low-density (LDPE) | Ethene | Flexible, transparent | Plastic bags, film wrap |\n| Poly(ethene) high-density (HDPE) | Ethene | Rigid, opaque | Bottles, pipes, crates |\n| Poly(propene) | Propene | Stiff, fatigue-resistant | Ropes, carpets, car parts |\n| PVC | Chloroethene | Rigid or flexible | Pipes, cables, raincoats |\n| Polystyrene | Styrene | Light, poor conductor | Packaging, disposable cups |\n| PTFE (Teflon) | Tetrafluoroethene | Very inert, low friction | Non-stick coatings, bearings |\n| Perspex | Methyl methacrylate | Transparent, light | Lenses, signs, windows |\n\n### Thermosetting Plastics\nThermosets harden permanently on heating due to **cross-links** between polymer chains. They cannot be remelted.\n\n| Polymer | Monomers | Uses |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bakelite | Phenol + formaldehyde | Electrical fittings, saucepan handles |\n| Melamine | Melamine + formaldehyde | Kitchenware, laminates |\n| Epoxy resin | Epoxide compounds | Adhesives, coatings |\n| Vulcanised rubber | Isoprene + sulphur cross-links | Tyres, hoses |\n\n### Natural Polymers\n\n**Rubber** (natural):\n- Source: Latex from rubber trees (Hevea brasiliensis) — important crop in Ghana, Cote d''Ivoire, Nigeria\n- Monomer: 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene (isoprene)\n- Properties: Elastic, soft, becomes sticky when hot\n- Vulcanisation: Heating with sulphur adds S-S cross-links, making rubber harder and more elastic\n\n**Starch and Cellulose:**\n- Both are polymers of glucose (monomer: C6H12O6)\n- Starch: alpha-glucose linkages — digestible (cassava, yam, plantain all rich in starch)\n- Cellulose: beta-glucose linkages — not digestible by humans; forms plant cell walls\n\n**Proteins:**\n- Monomers: 20 different amino acids\n- Joined by peptide bonds (-CO-NH-)\n- Examples: enzymes, haemoglobin, keratin (hair/nails), collagen\n\n### Structure vs Properties\n\n**Cross-linking increases:** hardness, rigidity, thermal stability, resistance to solvents\n\n**Long chain length increases:** strength and toughness\n\n**Branching decreases:** density and crystallinity (LDPE vs HDPE)\n\n**Polar side groups:** increase intermolecular forces and melting point\n\n## Environmental Impact of Plastics\n\n**Problems:**\n- Non-biodegradable: most plastics persist for 100-1000 years\n- Microplastics: fragments contaminate oceans, rivers, food chains\n- Toxic additives: plasticisers (phthalates), flame retardants leach into environment\n- Burning produces toxic gases: HCl (from PVC), dioxins\n\n**In West Africa:**\n- Sachet water bags (poly(ethene)) are a major litter problem in Ghana, Nigeria, Senegal\n- Open burning of plastics is common where waste collection is poor\n- Accra''s Agbogbloshie is one of the world''s largest e-waste sites\n\n**Solutions:**\n- Recycling (mechanical or chemical)\n- Biodegradable plastics (PLA from corn/cassava starch)\n- Bio-based plastics from agricultural waste\n- Legislation: Ghana banned lightweight plastic bags in 2015; Rwanda has one of Africa''s strictest plastic bans\n- Extended Producer Responsibility schemes\n\n**Recycling codes:**\n1 = PET (bottles), 2 = HDPE, 3 = PVC, 4 = LDPE, 5 = PP, 6 = PS, 7 = Other',
     'explanation', 2),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Definitions: Polymer Chemistry Terms',
     E'## Essential Polymer Chemistry Definitions\n\n**Polymer** - A large molecule made of many repeating structural units (monomers) joined by covalent bonds. From Greek: poly (many) + meros (part).\n\n**Monomer** - The small, simple molecule that is the repeating unit from which a polymer is built. Must have at least one reactive site (double bond or functional group).\n\n**Polymerisation** - The chemical process in which monomers combine to form a polymer.\n\n**Addition polymerisation** - Polymerisation in which monomers (containing C=C) join together without losing any atoms. Product has same empirical formula as monomer.\n\n**Condensation polymerisation** - Polymerisation in which monomers join with the loss of a small molecule (usually water) at each linkage.\n\n**Repeating unit** - The smallest group of atoms that, when repeated, gives the full polymer chain. Written in brackets with subscript n.\n\n**Degree of polymerisation (n)** - The number of repeating units in a polymer chain. Determines the molecular mass of the polymer.\n\n**Thermoplastic** - A polymer that softens on heating and can be remoulded. Has linear or branched chains; only weak intermolecular forces between chains.\n\n**Thermosetting plastic (thermoset)** - A polymer that permanently hardens on heating due to cross-links between chains. Cannot be remoulded.\n\n**Cross-linking** - Covalent bonds that join adjacent polymer chains, increasing rigidity and hardness.\n\n**Plasticiser** - A substance added to a polymer (e.g., PVC) to increase flexibility by spacing apart the polymer chains, reducing intermolecular forces.\n\n**Vulcanisation** - The process of heating natural rubber with sulphur to create sulphur cross-links, improving elasticity, hardness, and durability.\n\n**Amide bond (peptide bond)** - The linkage -CO-NH- formed between a carboxylic acid group (-COOH) and an amine group (-NH2) in condensation polymerisation.\n\n**Ester bond** - The linkage -COO- formed between a carboxylic acid (-COOH) and a hydroxyl (-OH) group in condensation polymerisation.\n\n**Biodegradable polymer** - A polymer that can be broken down by microorganisms into simpler substances (CO2, H2O, biomass).\n\n**Monomer unit of nylon 6,6** - Hexanedioic acid (adipic acid) and 1,6-diaminohexane\n\n**Monomer unit of Dacron/Terylene** - Benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (terephthalic acid) and ethane-1,2-diol',
     'definitions', 3),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Worked Examples: Polymer Identification and Drawing',
     E'## Worked Example 1: Identify the Type of Polymerisation\n\n**Question:** State the type of polymerisation and identify the monomer for each:\na) [-CH2-CF2-]n\nb) Nylon 6,6\nc) [-CH2-CH(CN)-]n\n\n**Solution:**\na) Addition polymerisation. Monomer = CH2=CF2 (1,1-difluoroethene). The repeating unit shows C-C backbone with F substituents, derived from an alkene.\n\nb) Condensation polymerisation. Monomers = HOOC-(CH2)4-COOH (hexanedioic acid) and H2N-(CH2)6-NH2 (1,6-diaminohexane). Amide bonds form with loss of H2O.\n\nc) Addition polymerisation. Monomer = CH2=CHCN (propenenitrile/acrylonitrile). Used to make polyacrylonitrile (acrylic fibres — used for knitwear common in West Africa).\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Draw the Repeating Unit\n\n**Question:** Draw the repeating unit of the polymer formed from but-1-ene.\n\n**Monomer:** CH2=CH-CH2-CH3 (but-1-ene)\n\n**Step 1:** Identify the double bond: between C1 and C2\n**Step 2:** Open the double bond — each carbon gains one free valence\n**Step 3:** Write the repeating unit:\n\n[-CH2-CH(CH2CH3)-]n\n\nOr drawn as: [-CH2-CHCH2CH3-]n with bonds extending left and right from the terminal carbons.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Deduce the Monomer from the Polymer\n\n**Question:** What is the monomer of PVC? Write the equation for its polymerisation.\n\n**Polymer:** [-CH2-CHCl-]n\n\n**Step 1:** Add a double bond between the two carbons in the repeating unit\n**Monomer:** CH2=CHCl (chloroethene / vinyl chloride)\n\n**Equation:** nCH2=CHCl --> [-CH2-CHCl-]n\n\n**Notes on PVC:**\n- Rigid PVC: used for pipes, window frames (no plasticiser)\n- Flexible PVC: used for cables, hoses (with plasticiser)\n- PVC tiles are common flooring in West African schools and offices\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Condensation Polymer Structure\n\n**Question:** Draw the structure of a section of nylon 6,6 and identify the type of linkage.\n\n**Monomers:**\n- Hexanedioic acid: HOOC-(CH2)4-COOH\n- 1,6-Diaminohexane: H2N-(CH2)6-NH2\n\n**Reaction:** -COOH + H2N- --> -CO-NH- + H2O\n\n**Section of nylon 6,6:**\n... -CO-(CH2)4-CO-NH-(CH2)6-NH-CO-(CH2)4-CO-NH-(CH2)6-NH- ...\n\n**Linkage:** Amide bond (-CO-NH-), also called peptide bond in proteins\n\n**Uses in West Africa:** Nylon is used for fishing nets along Ghana''s coast, fabric, ropes, and hosiery.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 5: Plastic Identification\n\n**Question:** A student finds a plastic bottle with the recycling code "1 PET". Explain what this means and whether the bottle is thermoplastic or thermosetting.\n\n**Answer:**\n- PET = Poly(ethylene terephthalate), a polyester\n- It is a condensation polymer of ethane-1,2-diol and terephthalic acid\n- Recycling code 1 indicates it is widely recyclable\n- PET bottles are thermoplastic — they can be melted and reshaped\n- When recycled, PET can be made into fibres for clothing (polyester fleece) or new bottles',
     'examples', 4),
    (v_topic_id,
     'WASSCE Practice: Polymers and Plastics',
     E'## WASSCE-Style Questions: Polymers and Plastics\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**1.** Which of the following is a condensation polymer?\nA) Poly(ethene)\nB) PVC\nC) Polystyrene\nD) Nylon 6,6\n**Answer: D** — Nylon 6,6 is formed by condensation of hexanedioic acid and 1,6-diaminohexane with loss of water.\n\n**2.** The repeating unit of a polymer is [-CH2-CH(CH3)-]n. What is the monomer?\nA) Ethene\nB) Propene\nC) But-1-ene\nD) But-2-ene\n**Answer: B** — Adding a double bond between C1 and C2 gives CH2=CHCH3 (propene).\n\n**3.** Vulcanisation of rubber involves:\nA) Addition of plasticisers\nB) Cross-linking with sulphur\nC) Condensation with formaldehyde\nD) Addition of carbon black\n**Answer: B** — Sulphur forms cross-links between isoprene polymer chains.\n\n**4.** Which polymer is used to make non-stick coatings on cooking pans?\nA) PVC\nB) Polystyrene\nC) PTFE\nD) Nylon\n**Answer: C** — PTFE (polytetrafluoroethene / Teflon) is highly inert and has a very low coefficient of friction.\n\n**5.** A plastic softens on heating and can be remoulded. It is classified as:\nA) Thermosetting\nB) Thermoplastic\nC) Elastomer\nD) Biopolymer\n**Answer: B**\n\n---\n\n### Section B: Structured Questions\n\n**Question 1:**\na) State TWO differences between addition and condensation polymerisation. [4 marks]\nb) Draw the repeating unit of the polymer formed from CH2=CCl2. [2 marks]\nc) Name the polymer in (b) and give ONE industrial use. [2 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na)\n- In addition polymerisation, no by-product is formed; in condensation polymerisation, a small molecule (usually water) is lost at each linkage. [2]\n- Addition polymerisation uses monomers with a C=C double bond; condensation uses monomers with two different functional groups (-COOH and -OH or -NH2). [2]\n\nb) Repeating unit: [-CH2-CCl2-]n (polyvinylidene chloride / PVDC) [2]\n\nc) Name: Poly(1,1-dichloroethene) or PVDC [1]\nUse: Food packaging wrap (Saran wrap); barrier coating to prevent moisture and oxygen entry [1]\n\n**Question 2:**\nNatural rubber is obtained from the Hevea brasiliensis tree.\na) Name the monomer of natural rubber. [1 mark]\nb) Describe the process of vulcanisation and explain how it changes the properties of rubber. [4 marks]\nc) State TWO environmental problems caused by plastic waste in West Africa and suggest ONE solution for each. [4 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene (isoprene) [1]\n\nb) Vulcanisation: natural rubber is heated with sulphur (140-180 degrees C) [1]. Sulphur atoms form covalent cross-links between adjacent polymer chains [1]. This makes the rubber harder and more rigid [1], more elastic (returns to original shape after stretching) [1], less sticky when hot, and more durable [accept any 2 property changes for 2 marks]\n\nc) Problem 1: Non-biodegradable sachet water bags/plastic bags clog drains and cause flooding in cities like Accra and Lagos. Solution: Government ban on lightweight plastic bags (as implemented in Ghana, Rwanda) [2]\nProblem 2: Open burning of plastic waste releases toxic gases (HCl, dioxins, CO) causing air pollution and health issues. Solution: Establish formal waste collection systems and recycling facilities in urban areas [2]',
     'practice', 5);
  END IF;

  /* ============================================================
     TOPIC 17: INDUSTRIAL AND AGRICULTURAL CHEMISTRY
     ============================================================ */
  SELECT id INTO v_topic_id FROM topics WHERE name ILIKE '%industrial%' OR name ILIKE '%agricultural%' LIMIT 1;
  IF v_topic_id IS NOT NULL THEN
    DELETE FROM topic_sections WHERE topic_id = v_topic_id;
    INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Industrial Processes: Haber, Contact, Solvay, Chlor-Alkali',
     E'## Major Industrial Chemical Processes\n\n### 1. The Haber Process (Nitrogen Fixation)\n**Purpose:** Manufacture of ammonia (NH3) for fertilisers and other chemicals\n**Equation:** N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g)   DeltaH = -92 kJ/mol\n\n**Conditions:**\n- Temperature: 400-450 degrees C (compromise — higher yield at lower T, but too slow)\n- Pressure: 200 atm (higher pressure favours products: 4 moles --> 2 moles)\n- Catalyst: Iron with K2O and Al2O3 promoters\n- Continuous process: unreacted N2 and H2 recycled\n\n**Raw Materials:**\n- Nitrogen: from fractional distillation of liquid air\n- Hydrogen: from steam reforming of natural gas: CH4 + H2O --> CO + 3H2\n  Then: CO + H2O --> CO2 + H2 (water-gas shift reaction)\n\n**Yield:** ~15-25% at equilibrium; unreacted gases recycled to increase overall yield to ~98%\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Contact Process (Sulphuric Acid)\n**Purpose:** Manufacture of sulphuric acid (H2SO4) — most produced industrial chemical\n\n**Three stages:**\n1. SO2 production: S + O2 --> SO2 (burning sulphur)\n   OR: 4FeS2 + 11O2 --> 2Fe2O3 + 8SO2 (roasting pyrite)\n2. SO2 to SO3: 2SO2 + O2 <=> 2SO3   DeltaH = -197 kJ/mol\n   - Conditions: 450 degrees C, 1-2 atm, V2O5 catalyst\n3. Absorption: SO3 + H2SO4 --> H2S2O7 (oleum)\n   Then: H2S2O7 + H2O --> 2H2SO4\n   (SO3 cannot be dissolved directly in water — too violent and forms acid mist)\n\n**Uses of H2SO4:**\n- Fertiliser production (superphosphates): Ca3(PO4)2 + 2H2SO4 --> Ca(H2PO4)2 + 2CaSO4\n- Lead-acid batteries\n- Paint and pigments\n- Petrol refining\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Solvay Process (Sodium Carbonate)\n**Purpose:** Manufacture of Na2CO3 (soda ash) and NaHCO3 (baking soda)\n\n**Overall equation:** 2NaCl + CaCO3 --> Na2CO3 + CaCl2\n(This is not directly feasible; the Solvay process achieves it indirectly)\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Saturate NaCl solution with NH3\n2. Pass CO2 through: NaCl + NH3 + CO2 + H2O --> NaHCO3 + NH4Cl\n   (NaHCO3 is less soluble — precipitates out)\n3. Heat NaHCO3: 2NaHCO3 --> Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2 (CO2 recycled)\n4. Recover NH3: 2NH4Cl + Ca(OH)2 --> CaCl2 + 2NH3 + 2H2O (NH3 recycled)\n\n**Uses of Na2CO3:** Glass making, soap and detergent production, paper\n\n---\n\n### 4. The Chlor-Alkali Process (Electrolysis of Brine)\n**Purpose:** Produce Cl2, H2, and NaOH from concentrated NaCl solution\n\n**At cathode (-):** 2H2O + 2e- --> H2 + 2OH-\n**At anode (+):** 2Cl- --> Cl2 + 2e-\n**Overall:** 2NaCl + 2H2O --> Cl2 + H2 + 2NaOH\n\n**Products and uses:**\n- Cl2: PVC production, water purification, bleach (NaOCl)\n- H2: Haber process, fuel cells, margarine production\n- NaOH: soap-making (saponification), paper, alumina production (Bayer process)\n\n**West African relevance:** NaOH is used in traditional soap-making (black soap) in Ghana and Nigeria using palm oil or shea butter.',
     'overview', 1),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Agricultural Chemistry: Fertilisers, Pesticides, and Soil Science',
     E'## Agricultural Chemistry for West Africa\n\n### Fertilisers\nFertilisers supply essential nutrients to plants for growth. The three macronutrients are:\n- **N** (Nitrogen): promotes leaf and stem growth; component of proteins and chlorophyll\n- **P** (Phosphorus): promotes root development and flowering; component of DNA and ATP\n- **K** (Potassium): regulates water balance, disease resistance, and enzyme function\n\n**NPK fertiliser**: Contains all three nutrients in specified ratios (e.g., 15:15:15)\n\n### Types of Fertilisers\n\n| Fertiliser | Formula | N% | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Ammonium nitrate | NH4NO3 | 35% | Most common; explosive if contaminated |\n| Urea | CO(NH2)2 | 46% | Highest N content; common in West Africa |\n| Ammonium sulphate | (NH4)2SO4 | 21% | Also provides sulphur |\n| Ammonium phosphate | (NH4)3PO4 | 18% | Provides N and P |\n| Superphosphate | Ca(H2PO4)2 | - | P fertiliser; made with H2SO4 |\n| Potassium chloride | KCl | - | Main K fertiliser (muriate of potash) |\n\n### Production of Ammonium Fertilisers\n1. Produce NH3 by Haber process\n2. React NH3 with acids:\n   - NH3 + HNO3 --> NH4NO3 (ammonium nitrate)\n   - 2NH3 + H2SO4 --> (NH4)2SO4 (ammonium sulphate)\n   - 3NH3 + H3PO4 --> (NH4)3PO4 (ammonium phosphate)\n3. Dry and granulate the salt\n\n### Eutrophication — Fertiliser Run-off Problem\n**Process:**\n1. Excess fertiliser applied to farms\n2. Rain washes nitrates/phosphates into rivers and lakes (leaching)\n3. Algae and water plants grow rapidly (algal bloom)\n4. Algae blocks light; plants below die\n5. Bacteria decompose dead plants — use up dissolved O2 (aerobic decomposition)\n6. O2 levels drop (deoxygenation)\n7. Fish and aquatic life die (fish kill)\n8. Water body becomes dead zone\n\n**Solutions:** Controlled fertiliser application, buffer strips, constructed wetlands\n\n### Pesticides\n\n| Type | Target | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Insecticides | Insects | DDT, organophosphates, pyrethroids |\n| Herbicides | Weeds | Glyphosate, 2,4-D (selective), paraquat |\n| Fungicides | Fungi | Copper sulphate (Bordeaux mixture), mancozeb |\n| Rodenticides | Rodents | Warfarin |\n\n**Selective vs Non-selective:**\n- Selective herbicides (2,4-D): kill broad-leaved weeds but not grasses (safe for maize, rice)\n- Non-selective (paraquat, glyphosate): kill all plants on contact\n\n**Problems with Pesticides:**\n- Bioaccumulation: DDT builds up in fatty tissue up the food chain\n- Non-target harm: bees, birds, soil organisms affected\n- Persistence: some pesticides remain in soil for years\n- Resistance development in pests\n- Water contamination\n\n**West African context:**\n- Cocoa farmers in Ghana use fungicides (mancozeb) to combat black pod disease\n- Rice farmers in the Volta basin use herbicides to control weeds\n- Cotton farmers in Burkina Faso and Mali are major pesticide users in the region\n\n### Soil Chemistry\n**pH and Agriculture:**\n- Optimum pH for most crops: 6.0-7.0 (slightly acidic to neutral)\n- Acidic soils (pH < 6): treated with lime (CaCO3 or Ca(OH)2)\n  CaCO3 + H2SO4 --> CaSO4 + H2O + CO2\n  Ca(OH)2 + 2HCl --> CaCl2 + 2H2O\n- Alkaline soils (pH > 7.5): treated with sulphur or acidic fertilisers\n\n**Tropical soils in West Africa:**\n- Often lateritic (rich in iron and aluminium oxides)\n- Can become acidic after leaching by heavy rains\n- Liming is practised in forest zones (southern Ghana, Cote d''Ivoire)',
     'explanation', 2),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Definitions: Industrial and Agricultural Chemistry',
     E'## Essential Definitions\n\n**Haber process** - The industrial synthesis of ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen gases using an iron catalyst at 400-450 degrees C and 200 atm pressure.\n\n**Contact process** - The industrial manufacture of sulphuric acid in three stages: production of SO2, catalytic oxidation of SO2 to SO3, and absorption of SO3 in sulphuric acid to give oleum, then dilution.\n\n**Chlor-alkali process** - The industrial process of electrolyzing concentrated sodium chloride (brine) solution to produce chlorine gas, hydrogen gas, and sodium hydroxide solution.\n\n**Solvay process** - Industrial manufacture of sodium carbonate (soda ash) from sodium chloride, limestone, and ammonia via a series of steps.\n\n**Fertiliser** - A substance applied to soil to supply essential plant nutrients (N, P, K) that may be deficient, promoting plant growth and crop yield.\n\n**NPK fertiliser** - A compound fertiliser containing the three major plant nutrients: nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P), and potassium (K).\n\n**Urea** - CO(NH2)2; the nitrogen fertiliser with the highest percentage nitrogen (46%) by mass; produced by reacting NH3 with CO2.\n\n**Eutrophication** - The process by which excess nutrients (nitrates/phosphates) entering a water body cause rapid algal growth, depletion of dissolved oxygen, and death of aquatic life.\n\n**Pesticide** - A chemical substance used to kill or control organisms considered harmful to crops or human health.\n\n**Herbicide** - A pesticide used to kill unwanted plants (weeds).\n\n**Insecticide** - A pesticide used to kill insects.\n\n**Bioaccumulation** - The progressive increase in concentration of a persistent chemical (e.g., DDT) in an organism''s body over time.\n\n**Biomagnification** - The increase in concentration of a persistent substance at each successive trophic level in a food chain.\n\n**Superphosphate** - The phosphate fertiliser Ca(H2PO4)2, made by reacting rock phosphate with sulphuric acid.\n\n**Liming** - Application of calcium carbonate or calcium hydroxide to acidic soils to raise the pH to optimal levels for crop growth.\n\n**Steam reforming** - Industrial process for producing hydrogen: methane (from natural gas) reacts with steam over a nickel catalyst at high temperature: CH4 + H2O --> CO + 3H2.\n\n**Oleum (fuming sulphuric acid)** - H2S2O7; formed when SO3 is dissolved in concentrated H2SO4; diluted with water to give sulphuric acid.\n\n**Saponification** - The reaction of a fat or oil with NaOH or KOH to produce soap (sodium/potassium salts of fatty acids) and glycerol.',
     'definitions', 3),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Worked Examples: Industrial Calculations and Process Analysis',
     E'## Worked Example 1: Haber Process Equilibrium Analysis\n\n**Question:** For the Haber process: N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g), DeltaH = -92 kJ/mol\n\nExplain why the following conditions are used:\na) Temperature of 400-450 degrees C\nb) Pressure of 200 atm\nc) Iron catalyst\n\n**Answer:**\na) Temperature 400-450 degrees C:\n- The forward reaction is exothermic (DeltaH = -92 kJ/mol)\n- By Le Chatelier''s principle, low temperature favours the forward reaction (gives higher yield of NH3)\n- However, too low a temperature makes the reaction too slow (insufficient activation energy)\n- 400-450 degrees C is a compromise: reasonable yield (~15-25%) AND reasonable rate\n\nb) Pressure of 200 atm:\n- Left side: 1 + 3 = 4 moles of gas; Right side: 2 moles of gas\n- High pressure shifts equilibrium to the right (fewer moles of gas) — increases yield\n- 200 atm gives ~25% yield; higher pressure (e.g., 1000 atm) would give more NH3 but\n  equipment cost and energy cost become too high\n- 200 atm is a compromise between yield and cost\n\nc) Iron catalyst:\n- The catalyst does NOT change the position of equilibrium or the yield\n- It increases the RATE at which equilibrium is reached\n- The catalytic iron provides an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy\n- This allows a lower temperature to be used, which is more economical\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Fertiliser Nitrogen Content Calculation\n\n**Question:** Calculate the percentage by mass of nitrogen in:\na) Ammonium nitrate, NH4NO3 (RAM: N=14, H=1, O=16)\nb) Urea, CO(NH2)2 (RAM: C=12, O=16, N=14, H=1)\n\n**Solution:**\n\na) Mr of NH4NO3 = 14 + 4(1) + 14 + 3(16) = 14 + 4 + 14 + 48 = 80\n   Mass of N in one formula unit = 14 + 14 = 28 (two N atoms)\n   % N = (28/80) x 100 = 35.0%\n\nb) Mr of CO(NH2)2 = 12 + 16 + 2(14) + 4(1) = 12 + 16 + 28 + 4 = 60\n   Mass of N = 2 x 14 = 28\n   % N = (28/60) x 100 = 46.7%\n\n**Conclusion:** Urea has the higher nitrogen content per unit mass, making it more cost-effective to transport.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Eutrophication Sequence\n\n**Question:** A rice farm in the Volta basin has excess ammonium nitrate fertiliser applied before heavy rains. Describe the sequence of events that leads to fish death in a nearby river.\n\n**Model Answer (6 marks):**\n1. Heavy rain washes excess nitrate and phosphate into the river (leaching) [1]\n2. The nutrient-rich water causes rapid growth of algae and aquatic plants (algal bloom) [1]\n3. The thick algal layer on the water surface blocks sunlight from reaching plants below [1]\n4. Submerged plants cannot photosynthesize and die [1]\n5. Bacteria decompose the dead plants using aerobic respiration, rapidly consuming dissolved oxygen [1]\n6. Dissolved oxygen concentration falls to very low levels; fish and other aquatic organisms suffocate and die (deoxygenation) [1]\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Contact Process Conditions\n\n**Question:** In the Contact process: 2SO2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2SO3(g), DeltaH = -197 kJ/mol\n\nWhy is SO3 NOT dissolved directly in water to make sulphuric acid?\n\n**Answer:**\nSO3 + H2O --> H2SO4 would release a very large amount of heat rapidly.\nThis produces a dense white mist/fog of fine H2SO4 droplets that would be very difficult to condense and would escape into the atmosphere as an air pollutant (acid mist).\nInstead, SO3 is dissolved in concentrated H2SO4 to form oleum (H2S2O7), which is then safely diluted with water to give H2SO4.',
     'examples', 4),
    (v_topic_id,
     'WASSCE Practice: Industrial and Agricultural Chemistry',
     E'## WASSCE-Style Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**1.** Which conditions are used in the Haber process?\nA) 450 degrees C, 200 atm, V2O5 catalyst\nB) 450 degrees C, 200 atm, Fe catalyst\nC) 600 degrees C, 1 atm, Fe catalyst\nD) 200 degrees C, 200 atm, Pt catalyst\n**Answer: B**\n\n**2.** Which fertiliser has the highest percentage of nitrogen by mass?\nA) Ammonium sulphate (21%)\nB) Ammonium nitrate (35%)\nC) Urea (46%)\nD) Potassium nitrate (13%)\n**Answer: C**\n\n**3.** In the Contact process, SO3 is absorbed in concentrated H2SO4 rather than water because:\nA) Water is too expensive\nB) Dissolving in water produces a dangerous acid mist\nC) SO3 does not react with water\nD) The yield of H2SO4 would be lower\n**Answer: B**\n\n**4.** Eutrophication of a lake is caused by:\nA) Oil spillage from ships\nB) Excess nutrients from agricultural run-off\nC) Discharge of heavy metals from mines\nD) Acid rain from sulphur dioxide\n**Answer: B**\n\n**5.** Liming is applied to agricultural soils to:\nA) Add nitrogen fertiliser\nB) Increase soil acidity\nC) Neutralise excess acidity\nD) Kill soil bacteria\n**Answer: C**\n\n---\n\n### Section B: Structured Questions\n\n**Question 1 (WASSCE style):**\na) Outline the three stages of the Contact process for the manufacture of sulphuric acid. Include equations for any reactions that occur. [6 marks]\n\nb) Give TWO large-scale industrial uses of sulphuric acid. [2 marks]\n\nc) Explain why, in terms of Le Chatelier''s principle, a moderately high temperature of 450 degrees C is used in stage 2, rather than a very low temperature. [3 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na)\nStage 1 — Production of SO2:\nS(s) + O2(g) --> SO2(g) [or 4FeS2 + 11O2 --> 2Fe2O3 + 8SO2 if using pyrite] [2]\n\nStage 2 — Oxidation of SO2 to SO3:\n2SO2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2SO3(g); conditions: 450 degrees C, 1-2 atm, V2O5 catalyst [2]\n\nStage 3 — Absorption and dilution:\nSO3 is dissolved in concentrated H2SO4 to form oleum: SO3 + H2SO4 --> H2S2O7 [1]\nOleum is then diluted carefully with water: H2S2O7 + H2O --> 2H2SO4 [1]\n\nb) Any two from: manufacture of fertilisers (superphosphates); lead-acid batteries; petroleum refining; manufacture of detergents/paints/plastics/dyes [2]\n\nc) The forward reaction (SO2 --> SO3) is exothermic (DeltaH = -197 kJ/mol) [1]. By Le Chatelier''s principle, lowering temperature shifts equilibrium to the right, giving higher yield of SO3 [1]. However, too low a temperature makes the reaction too slow; 450 degrees C is a compromise between yield and speed of reaction [1].\n\n**Question 2:**\nA farmer in the Northern Region of Ghana grows maize on acidic soil (pH 5.2) and wants to apply fertiliser.\na) Suggest a suitable substance to apply before fertilising. Write an equation for its reaction with the acid in soil. [3 marks]\nb) Name a suitable nitrogen fertiliser and calculate its percentage N content. (RAM: N=14, H=1, S=32, O=16) [3 marks]\nc) Describe the environmental problem that results from over-application of this fertiliser near a river. [4 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) Calcium carbonate (limestone) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime) [1]\nCaCO3 + H2SO4 --> CaSO4 + H2O + CO2 [1] OR Ca(OH)2 + 2HCl --> CaCl2 + 2H2O [1]\n\nb) Ammonium sulphate, (NH4)2SO4 [1]\nMr = (14+4) x 2 + 32 + 64 = 36 + 32 + 64 = 132\nMass of N = 2 x 14 = 28\n% N = (28/132) x 100 = 21.2% [2]\n\nc) Excess fertiliser is washed by rain into the river (leaching) [1]; nitrates/phosphates cause rapid algal growth (algal bloom) [1]; algae blocks sunlight, plants below die; bacteria decompose dead organic matter [1]; dissolved oxygen is depleted; fish and aquatic life die from deoxygenation [1]',
     'practice', 5);
  END IF;

  /* ============================================================
     TOPIC 18: ENVIRONMENTAL CHEMISTRY
     ============================================================ */
  SELECT id INTO v_topic_id FROM topics WHERE name ILIKE '%environment%' LIMIT 1;
  IF v_topic_id IS NOT NULL THEN
    DELETE FROM topic_sections WHERE topic_id = v_topic_id;
    INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
    (v_topic_id,
     'Air Pollution: Causes, Effects and Control',
     E'## Environmental Chemistry: Air Pollution\n\n### Major Air Pollutants\n\n| Pollutant | Sources | Effects | Control |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Carbon monoxide (CO) | Incomplete combustion of fuels; car exhaust | Binds to haemoglobin (carboxyhaemoglobin); reduces O2 transport; lethal in high concentrations | Catalytic converters; better engine design |\n| Carbon dioxide (CO2) | Complete combustion; respiration | Greenhouse gas; global warming; ocean acidification | Renewable energy; carbon capture; reforestation |\n| Sulphur dioxide (SO2) | Burning coal and oil with sulphur; volcanic activity | Acid rain (H2SO4); respiratory irritant; kills plants | Use low-sulphur fuels; flue gas desulphurisation |\n| Nitrogen oxides (NOx) | High-temperature combustion (cars, power stations) | Acid rain (HNO3); photochemical smog; respiratory damage | Catalytic converters; lower combustion temperatures |\n| Particulate matter (soot) | Incomplete combustion; Saharan dust | Respiratory disease; reduces visibility; global dimming | Filters; electrostatic precipitators |\n| Methane (CH4) | Livestock, rice paddies, landfills, natural gas leaks | Potent greenhouse gas (25x CO2 over 100 years) | Capture landfill gas; reduce livestock numbers |\n| CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons) | Old refrigerants, aerosols | Destroy stratospheric ozone layer | Replaced by HFCs under Montreal Protocol |\n| Lead (Pb) | Leaded petrol (now phased out) | Neurotoxin; damages developing brain in children | Unleaded petrol; phase-out (complete in most of West Africa) |\n\n### Acid Rain\n**Formation of acid rain from SO2:**\nSO2 + H2O --> H2SO3 (sulphurous acid)\n2SO2 + O2 --> 2SO3 (in atmosphere)\nSO3 + H2O --> H2SO4 (sulphuric acid)\n\n**Formation of acid rain from NOx:**\n4NO + 3O2 + 2H2O --> 4HNO3 (nitric acid)\n\n**pH of acid rain:** Below 5.6 (normal rain is slightly acidic pH 5.6 due to dissolved CO2)\n\n**Effects of acid rain:**\n- Damages forests and kills trees (soil acidification removes Ca2+, Mg2+ nutrients)\n- Acidifies lakes and rivers; kills fish (aluminium ions released from soil are toxic to fish)\n- Corrodes limestone buildings and statues: CaCO3 + H2SO4 --> CaSO4 + H2O + CO2\n- Damages metals, particularly iron (accelerates rusting)\n\n**Control:**\n- Flue gas desulphurisation (FGD): CaO + SO2 --> CaSO3 (limestone scrubbing)\n- Catalytic converters convert NOx: 2CO + 2NO --> N2 + 2CO2\n- Use of cleaner fuels (natural gas instead of coal)\n\n### The Ozone Layer\n**Stratospheric ozone (O3) formation:**\nO2 + UV radiation --> 2O (atomic oxygen)\nO + O2 --> O3 (ozone)\n\n**Ozone depletion by CFCs:**\nCCl3F --> Cl + CCl2F (UV radiation breaks C-Cl bond)\nCl + O3 --> ClO + O2 (chain reaction — one Cl atom can destroy 100,000 ozone molecules)\nClO + O --> Cl + O2 (Cl regenerated)\n\n**Effects of ozone depletion:**\n- Increased UV-B radiation reaching Earth''s surface\n- Increased skin cancer and cataracts in humans\n- Damage to phytoplankton and marine ecosystems\n\n**Montreal Protocol (1987):** International agreement to phase out CFCs; replaced with HFCs (hydrochlorofluorocarbons) and HFOs.\n\n### Greenhouse Effect and Global Warming\n**Greenhouse gases:** CO2, CH4, N2O, H2O vapour, CFCs\n\n**Mechanism:**\n1. Solar radiation (short wavelength) passes through atmosphere\n2. Earth absorbs and re-emits as infrared radiation (long wavelength)\n3. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared — trapping heat\n4. Enhanced greenhouse effect due to increased GHG from human activity\n\n**Effects on West Africa:**\n- Sahel desertification and reduced rainfall\n- Coastal flooding (sea level rise threatens Accra, Lagos, Cotonou)\n- Disruption of farming seasons; crop failures\n- Increased malaria range as temperature rises\n\n**Mitigation:** Reduce fossil fuel use; reforestation (REDD+ programmes in West African forests); renewable energy',
     'overview', 1),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Water Pollution, Treatment and Conservation',
     E'## Water Pollution\n\n### Major Water Pollutants\n\n| Pollutant | Sources | Effects |\n|---|---|---|\n| Nitrates/Phosphates | Agricultural fertiliser run-off | Eutrophication; algal blooms; deoxygenation |\n| Heavy metals (Pb, Hg, Cd, As) | Mining; industrial effluent; leaded paint | Bioaccumulation; neurotoxic; carcinogenic |\n| Oil | Spills; industrial discharge | Smothers aquatic life; kills birds; destroys beaches |\n| Pathogens (bacteria, viruses) | Untreated sewage; animal waste | Cholera, typhoid, dysentery |\n| Organic waste | Sewage; food processing | Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD) increases; fish die |\n| Chlorinated solvents | Industrial cleaning | Carcinogenic; persist in groundwater |\n| Thermal pollution | Power station cooling water | Reduces dissolved O2; kills fish; encourages algae |\n| Plastic debris | Littering; storm drains | Entangles wildlife; microplastics in food chain |\n\n**West African Water Issues:**\n- Artisanal gold mining (galamsey) in Ghana pollutes rivers with mercury (Hg) and cyanide\n- Oil spills in Niger Delta (Nigeria) have devastated mangroves and fishing communities\n- Sachet water culture generates massive plastic waste in Accra, Lagos, Kumasi\n- Volta Lake and Niger River contaminated by agricultural run-off\n- Many rural communities lack access to clean drinking water\n\n### Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD)\n- **Definition:** The amount of oxygen (in mg/L) needed by microorganisms to decompose organic matter in a water sample over 5 days at 20 degrees C\n- Low BOD (< 2 mg/L): clean water\n- High BOD (> 8 mg/L): heavily polluted water\n- BOD is used to assess effectiveness of sewage treatment\n\n### Water Treatment (Making Drinking Water Safe)\n\n**Stage 1 — Screening:** Large debris (leaves, sticks) removed by metal screens\n\n**Stage 2 — Sedimentation:** Water held in settling tanks; large suspended particles sink\n\n**Stage 3 — Coagulation/Flocculation:**\n- Aluminium sulphate (alum, Al2(SO4)3) or iron(III) sulphate added\n- Al3+ ions cause colloidal particles to clump together (flocculate) into larger flocs\n- Flocs settle out, removing fine clay, bacteria, and organic matter\n\n**Stage 4 — Filtration:**\n- Water passes through sand and gravel beds\n- Removes remaining suspended particles and some bacteria\n\n**Stage 5 — Disinfection:**\n- Chlorine gas or sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl) added\n- Kills pathogens: bacteria, viruses, protozoa\n- Chlorine dose carefully controlled (too little: pathogens survive; too much: chlorinated byproducts may be harmful)\n- Alternative: ozone (O3) treatment, UV radiation\n\n**Stage 6 — pH adjustment:**\n- Lime (Ca(OH)2) added to raise pH to 6.8-7.2 (reduces pipe corrosion)\n\n**Stage 7 — Fluoridation (some countries):**\n- NaF added (0.5-1.0 mg/L) to reduce tooth decay\n\n### Sewage Treatment\n**Primary treatment:** Physical removal of solids (screening, sedimentation)\n**Secondary treatment:** Biological oxidation by aerobic bacteria (trickling filters or activated sludge) — removes dissolved organic matter\n**Tertiary treatment:** Removal of nitrates, phosphates; disinfection; polishing\n\n### Hard Water Chemistry\n**Temporary hardness:** Ca(HCO3)2 (calcium hydrogencarbonate)\n- Removed by boiling: Ca(HCO3)2 --> CaCO3 + H2O + CO2\n- Forms limescale (CaCO3) in pipes and kettles\n\n**Permanent hardness:** CaSO4 or MgSO4\n- Cannot be removed by boiling\n- Removed by ion exchange resin or by adding Na2CO3 (washing soda): Ca2+ + CO3 2- --> CaCO3',
     'explanation', 2),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Definitions: Environmental Chemistry',
     E'## Essential Environmental Chemistry Definitions\n\n**Pollutant** - A substance that is present in the environment at a level that is harmful to living organisms or the physical environment.\n\n**Air pollution** - The presence of harmful substances (gases, particles) in the atmosphere above natural background levels.\n\n**Acid rain** - Precipitation with a pH below 5.6, formed when SO2 and NOx dissolve in atmospheric moisture to form sulphuric and nitric acids.\n\n**Greenhouse effect** - The natural warming of Earth''s surface caused by greenhouse gases (CO2, CH4, H2O, N2O) absorbing and re-emitting infrared radiation.\n\n**Enhanced greenhouse effect** - The increase in the natural greenhouse effect due to increased concentrations of greenhouse gases from human activities.\n\n**Global warming** - The rise in average global temperature due to the enhanced greenhouse effect.\n\n**Ozone layer** - A region of the stratosphere (15-35 km above Earth) containing relatively high concentrations of ozone (O3), which absorbs harmful UV-B radiation.\n\n**Ozone depletion** - The thinning of the stratospheric ozone layer caused by reactions with CFCs, leading to increased UV radiation at the surface.\n\n**CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons)** - Synthetic compounds containing C, F, and Cl; used as refrigerants and aerosol propellants; destroy stratospheric ozone.\n\n**Eutrophication** - Excessive enrichment of a water body with nutrients (nitrates/phosphates), leading to algal blooms, depletion of dissolved oxygen, and death of aquatic life.\n\n**BOD (Biological Oxygen Demand)** - A measure of the amount of dissolved oxygen needed by microorganisms to decompose organic matter in water; indicator of pollution level.\n\n**Bioaccumulation** - The gradual build-up of a persistent substance (e.g., DDT, mercury) in the tissues of an organism over time.\n\n**Biomagnification** - The increase in concentration of a persistent pollutant at successive levels of the food chain.\n\n**Flocculation** - The process of adding a chemical (e.g., alum) to water to cause fine suspended particles to clump together into larger aggregates (flocs) that settle out.\n\n**Coagulation** - The destabilisation of colloidal particles by addition of an electrolyte (alum) so they can aggregate.\n\n**Chlorination** - Addition of chlorine or chlorine compounds to water to kill pathogenic microorganisms.\n\n**Hard water** - Water containing dissolved Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions that react with soap to form scum and prevent lathering.\n\n**Temporary hardness** - Hardness caused by Ca(HCO3)2; removed by boiling.\n\n**Permanent hardness** - Hardness caused by CaSO4 or MgSO4; cannot be removed by boiling.\n\n**Photochemical smog** - Air pollution formed when sunlight reacts with NOx and hydrocarbons to produce ozone and other oxidants at ground level.\n\n**Carbon footprint** - The total amount of greenhouse gases (expressed as CO2 equivalent) emitted directly or indirectly by an individual, organisation, or activity.',
     'definitions', 3),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Worked Examples: Environmental Calculations and Analysis',
     E'## Worked Example 1: Acid Rain Formation\n\n**Question:** Describe how sulphur dioxide from burning coal leads to acid rain. Write equations for all reactions.\n\n**Answer:**\n1. Coal containing sulphur burns:\n   S(s) + O2(g) --> SO2(g) [formation of sulphur dioxide]\n\n2. SO2 dissolves in rainwater:\n   SO2(g) + H2O(l) --> H2SO3(aq) [sulphurous acid — weak acid]\n\n3. SO2 is further oxidised in the atmosphere:\n   2SO2(g) + O2(g) --> 2SO3(g) [with catalysts: dust, soot, O3]\n\n4. SO3 dissolves in water:\n   SO3(g) + H2O(l) --> H2SO4(aq) [sulphuric acid — strong acid]\n\n5. Rain containing H2SO3 and H2SO4 falls as acid rain (pH < 5.6)\n\n6. Acid rain corrodes limestone buildings:\n   CaCO3(s) + H2SO4(aq) --> CaSO4(s) + H2O(l) + CO2(g)\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: BOD Interpretation\n\n**Question:** River water samples were collected upstream (A) and downstream (B) of a sewage discharge point. Dissolved oxygen was measured before and after 5 days of incubation at 20 degrees C:\n- Sample A: Initial O2 = 9.0 mg/L; Final O2 = 8.1 mg/L; BOD = 0.9 mg/L\n- Sample B: Initial O2 = 9.0 mg/L; Final O2 = 1.5 mg/L; BOD = 7.5 mg/L\n\nInterpret these results and explain what they suggest about water quality.\n\n**Answer:**\n- Sample A (upstream): BOD = 0.9 mg/L. This is very low, indicating clean water with very little organic matter. Few bacteria are active; the river is in good ecological condition.\n\n- Sample B (downstream): BOD = 7.5 mg/L. This is high, indicating heavy organic pollution from the sewage discharge. Large numbers of aerobic bacteria are actively decomposing organic waste, consuming dissolved oxygen rapidly. At this dissolved O2 level (1.5 mg/L), sensitive fish species would be stressed or dead. The water is heavily polluted and unsafe.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Water Treatment\n\n**Question:** Describe the role of aluminium sulphate (alum) in water treatment. What chemistry is involved?\n\n**Answer:**\nWhen Al2(SO4)3 is added to water, it dissolves and the Al3+ ions react with water molecules:\nAl3+(aq) + 3H2O --> Al(OH)3(s) + 3H+(aq)\n\nAl(OH)3 forms a gelatinous (jelly-like) precipitate. This positively charged colloid attracts negatively charged colloidal particles in the water (clay, organic matter, bacteria) — this is coagulation.\n\nThe small clumps combine to form larger, heavier aggregates (flocs) — this is flocculation. The heavy flocs sink to the bottom of the settling tank, removing fine particles that could not be removed by simple sedimentation.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Ozone Depletion Chain Reaction\n\n**Question:** Explain, using equations, how CFCs destroy stratospheric ozone in a chain reaction.\n\n**Answer:**\nStep 1 — Initiation (UV breaks C-Cl bond in CFC):\nCCl2F2(g) --> CClF2(g) + Cl(g)\n\nStep 2 — Propagation (chain reaction):\nCl(g) + O3(g) --> ClO(g) + O2(g)\nClO(g) + O(g) --> Cl(g) + O2(g)\n\nThe chlorine atom is regenerated and reacts with more ozone — each Cl atom can destroy up to 100,000 ozone molecules before it is eventually removed from the catalytic cycle.\n\nOverall: O3 + O --> 2O2 (net destruction of ozone)\n\n**Consequences:** Increased UV-B radiation reaches Earth''s surface, increasing rates of skin cancer and cataracts, and damaging marine phytoplankton.',
     'examples', 4),
    (v_topic_id,
     'WASSCE Practice: Environmental Chemistry',
     E'## WASSCE-Style Questions: Environmental Chemistry\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**1.** Which gas is responsible for the depletion of the ozone layer?\nA) Carbon dioxide\nB) Sulphur dioxide\nC) Chlorofluorocarbon (CFC)\nD) Nitrogen gas\n**Answer: C**\n\n**2.** Acid rain is formed when SO2 and NOx dissolve in atmospheric moisture. What is the pH of acid rain?\nA) Above 7\nB) Exactly 7\nC) Between 5.6 and 7\nD) Below 5.6\n**Answer: D**\n\n**3.** The purpose of adding chlorine to drinking water is to:\nA) Remove hardness\nB) Kill pathogenic microorganisms\nC) Remove nitrates\nD) Lower the pH\n**Answer: B**\n\n**4.** Which of the following is the correct sequence in conventional water treatment?\nA) Chlorination, filtration, sedimentation, coagulation\nB) Coagulation, sedimentation, filtration, chlorination\nC) Filtration, chlorination, coagulation, sedimentation\nD) Sedimentation, coagulation, chlorination, filtration\n**Answer: B**\n\n**5.** The BOD of water from a river near a sewage outlet is found to be 12 mg/L. This indicates:\nA) Very clean water\nB) Moderately polluted water\nC) Heavily polluted water with high organic content\nD) Water with high dissolved oxygen\n**Answer: C**\n\n---\n\n### Section B: Structured Questions\n\n**Question 1:**\na) Explain the enhanced greenhouse effect and name TWO greenhouse gases. [4 marks]\nb) State TWO specific effects of global warming on West African countries. [2 marks]\nc) Suggest TWO ways that West African governments can reduce greenhouse gas emissions. [2 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) The Sun''s radiation (mainly visible and UV) passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth''s surface [1]. The Earth re-emits this energy as infrared (heat) radiation [1]. Greenhouse gases absorb this infrared radiation and re-emit it in all directions, trapping heat in the atmosphere [1]. Human activities (burning fossil fuels, deforestation) have increased the concentration of greenhouse gases, enhancing this effect and causing global warming [1].\nGreenhouse gases: CO2, CH4, N2O, H2O vapour, CFCs (any two) [0.5 each]\n\nb) Any two from: coastal flooding (Lagos, Accra, Cotonou at risk from sea level rise); desertification of the Sahel; disruption of rainfall patterns causing droughts/floods; crop failures; spread of tropical diseases like malaria to higher altitudes [1 each]\n\nc) Any two from: invest in solar/wind/hydroelectric power (Ghana has the Akosombo Dam); reduce deforestation; promote public transport; impose carbon taxes; improve energy efficiency in industry [1 each]\n\n**Question 2:**\na) Write equations to show how SO2 in the atmosphere leads to acid rain. [3 marks]\nb) Give TWO harmful effects of acid rain. [2 marks]\nc) Flue gas desulphurisation (FGD) uses calcium oxide to remove SO2 from power station emissions. Write an equation for this reaction. [1 mark]\nd) Why is mercury pollution in rivers of particular concern in gold-mining regions like Ghana? [3 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) SO2 + H2O --> H2SO3 [1]; 2SO2 + O2 --> 2SO3 [1]; SO3 + H2O --> H2SO4 [1]\n\nb) Any two: damages/kills trees by acidifying soil and removing mineral nutrients; acidifies lakes and rivers, killing fish; corrodes limestone buildings/statues/monuments; damages iron structures (accelerates rusting) [1 each]\n\nc) CaO + SO2 --> CaSO3 [1] (or CaCO3 + SO2 --> CaSO3 + CO2)\n\nd) Mercury (Hg) used in artisanal gold mining (galamsey) enters rivers as liquid mercury or methyl mercury [1]. Mercury bioaccumulates in fish tissue and biomagnifies up the food chain [1]. Humans consuming contaminated fish accumulate high mercury levels, causing neurological damage (Minamata disease), kidney failure, and developmental problems in children [1]',
     'practice', 5);
  END IF;

