    INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
    (v_topic_id,
     'Tests for Cations: Metal Ion Identification',
     E'## Qualitative Analysis: Identifying Cations\n\nQualitative analysis uses chemical tests to identify the ions present in an unknown sample. WASSCE candidates must master the tests for common cations and anions.\n\n### Tests for Metal Cations Using NaOH Solution\n\nAdd dilute NaOH solution to a solution of the unknown salt:\n\n| Ion | Observation with dilute NaOH | Observation with excess NaOH | Ionic Equation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Al3+ | White gelatinous precipitate | Dissolves in excess (amphoteric) | Al3+ + 3OH- --> Al(OH)3; then Al(OH)3 + OH- --> Al(OH)4- |\n| Ca2+ | White precipitate (slight) | Insoluble in excess | Ca2+ + 2OH- --> Ca(OH)2 |\n| Cu2+ | Blue precipitate | Insoluble in excess | Cu2+ + 2OH- --> Cu(OH)2 |\n| Fe2+ | Green precipitate | Insoluble; turns brown at surface (oxidised) | Fe2+ + 2OH- --> Fe(OH)2 |\n| Fe3+ | Red-brown precipitate | Insoluble in excess | Fe3+ + 3OH- --> Fe(OH)3 |\n| Mg2+ | White precipitate | Insoluble in excess | Mg2+ + 2OH- --> Mg(OH)2 |\n| Mn2+ | Cream/off-white precipitate | Insoluble | Mn2+ + 2OH- --> Mn(OH)2 |\n| Pb2+ | White precipitate | Dissolves in excess (amphoteric) | Pb2+ + 2OH- --> Pb(OH)2; then Pb(OH)2 + 2OH- --> [Pb(OH)4]2- |\n| Zn2+ | White precipitate | Dissolves in excess (amphoteric) | Zn2+ + 2OH- --> Zn(OH)2; then Zn(OH)2 + 2OH- --> [Zn(OH)4]2- |\n| NH4+ | No precipitate; warm and smell; pungent gas turns moist red litmus blue | | NH4+ + OH- --> NH3 + H2O |\n| Na+, K+ | No precipitate (soluble) | No change | |\n\n### Tests for Metal Cations Using Ammonia Solution (NH3 aq)\n\n| Ion | With dilute NH3 | With excess NH3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Al3+ | White precipitate | Insoluble in excess |\n| Cu2+ | Blue precipitate | Dissolves — deep blue solution [Cu(NH3)4]2+ |\n| Fe2+ | Green precipitate | Insoluble |\n| Fe3+ | Red-brown precipitate | Insoluble |\n| Mg2+ | White precipitate | Insoluble |\n| Pb2+ | White precipitate | Insoluble |\n| Zn2+ | White precipitate | Dissolves — colourless solution [Zn(NH3)4]2+ |\n\n### Flame Tests for Metal Ions\n\n| Metal ion | Flame colour |\n|---|---|\n| Lithium (Li+) | Crimson/scarlet red |\n| Sodium (Na+) | Persistent bright yellow-orange |\n| Potassium (K+) | Lilac/violet (seen through blue cobalt glass) |\n| Calcium (Ca2+) | Brick red |\n| Barium (Ba2+) | Apple green |\n| Copper (Cu2+) | Blue-green |\n| Strontium (Sr2+) | Scarlet red |\n\n**Technique:**\n1. Clean a platinum/nichrome wire loop with HCl and hold in blue Bunsen flame until no colour\n2. Dip clean loop into sample (solid or solution)\n3. Hold in the blue (non-luminous) part of the flame\n4. Observe and record the colour\n\n### Additional Cation Tests\n\n**Test for Fe3+:** Add potassium thiocyanate (KSCN) solution\n- Fe3+ + 3SCN- --> Fe(SCN)3 (blood-red colouration)\n\n**Test for Fe2+:** Add K3[Fe(CN)6] (potassium hexacyanoferrate(III))\n- Fe2+ + [Fe(CN)6]3- --> blue precipitate (Turnbull''s blue)\n\n**Test for Cu2+:** Add K4[Fe(CN)6] (potassium hexacyanoferrate(II))\n- Cu2+ gives chocolate-brown/reddish-brown precipitate',
     'overview', 1),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Tests for Anions and Gases',
     E'## Tests for Common Anions\n\n### Tests Using Dilute HCl or H2SO4\n\n| Anion | Test | Observation |\n|---|---|---|\n| CO3 2- (carbonate) | Add dilute HCl | Effervescence; colourless gas that turns limewater milky (CO2) |\n| HCO3- (hydrogencarbonate) | Add dilute HCl | Same as carbonate; also add to cold NaOH — no effervescence |\n| SO3 2- (sulphite) | Add dilute HCl | Pungent choking gas (SO2); turns acidified K2Cr2O7 green |\n| NO3- (nitrate) | "Brown ring test": add FeSO4, then conc. H2SO4 carefully | Brown ring at junction of liquids |\n\n### Tests Using AgNO3 Solution (acidified with dilute HNO3)\n\n| Anion | Observation | Solubility in NH3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cl- (chloride) | White precipitate of AgCl | Soluble in dilute NH3 |\n| Br- (bromide) | Cream precipitate of AgBr | Soluble only in conc. NH3 |\n| I- (iodide) | Yellow precipitate of AgI | Insoluble in NH3 |\n| SO4 2- | No precipitate with AgNO3; use BaCl2 instead | — |\n\n**Why acidify with HNO3?** To prevent precipitation of Ag2CO3 or Ag3PO4 which could give misleading results.\n\n### Tests Using BaCl2 Solution\n\n| Anion | Test | Observation |\n|---|---|---|\n| SO4 2- | Add BaCl2 + dilute HCl | White precipitate of BaSO4 (insoluble in excess HCl) |\n| SO3 2- | Add BaCl2 | White precipitate of BaSO3 (soluble in HCl — distinguishes from SO4 2-) |\n\n**Key distinction:** BaSO4 is insoluble in dilute HCl; BaSO3 dissolves in HCl (with SO2 gas). This test distinguishes sulphate from sulphite.\n\n### Tests for Other Anions\n\n| Anion | Test | Observation |\n|---|---|---|\n| PO4 3- | Add ammonium molybdate in dilute HNO3, warm | Yellow precipitate of ammonium phosphomolybdate |\n| NO2- | Add dilute H2SO4; acidified KMnO4 | Decolourises KMnO4 |\n| OH- | Universal indicator; red litmus paper | Blue colour |\n| I- | Add chlorine water and then organic solvent (cyclohexane); shake | Organic layer turns violet/purple |\n\n## Tests for Gases\n\n| Gas | Test | Observation |\n|---|---|---|\n| H2 | Lit splint | "Pop" / squeaky pop sound |\n| O2 | Glowing splint | Splint relights |\n| CO2 | Bubble through limewater (Ca(OH)2) | Turns milky/cloudy (CaCO3 formed) |\n| NH3 | Moist red litmus paper | Turns blue (alkaline) |\n| Cl2 | Moist blue litmus paper; starch-iodide paper | Turns red then bleaches; starch-iodide turns blue-black |\n| SO2 | Acidified K2Cr2O7 paper; acidified KMnO4 | Orange to green; decolourises KMnO4 |\n| HCl (and HBr, HI) | Damp litmus; NH3 fumes | Turns red; dense white fumes of NH4Cl |\n| H2S | Lead acetate paper | Turns black (PbS) |\n| NO2 | Brown gas; moist litmus | Turns red; brown-orange gas |\n| CO | Lit splint; no other obvious test | Burns with blue flame |\n\n### Equations for Gas Tests\n- Limewater: Ca(OH)2(aq) + CO2(g) --> CaCO3(s) + H2O(l)\n  Excess CO2: CaCO3(s) + CO2(g) + H2O(l) --> Ca(HCO3)2(aq) (milky --> clear again)\n- NH3 test: HCl gas + NH3(g) --> NH4Cl(s) (white smoke)\n- H2S test: Pb(CH3COO)2(aq) + H2S(g) --> PbS(s) + 2CH3COOH (black precipitate)\n\n## Systematic Identification Procedure\n1. Observe colour of solution/solid\n2. Perform flame test\n3. Add dilute HCl (effervescence = CO3 2- or SO3 2- or carbonate present)\n4. Add NaOH (identify cation by precipitate colour and solubility in excess)\n5. Add NH3 solution (compare with NaOH results)\n6. Add AgNO3 + HNO3 (identify halide)\n7. Add BaCl2 + HCl (identify sulphate)\n8. Confirm with additional specific tests',
     'explanation', 2),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Definitions: Qualitative Analysis Terms',
     E'## Essential Qualitative Analysis Definitions\n\n**Qualitative analysis** - Chemical tests used to identify which substances or ions are present in an unknown sample, without measuring amounts.\n\n**Quantitative analysis** - Determining the amount (concentration or mass) of a substance in a sample.\n\n**Cation** - A positively charged ion formed when an atom loses one or more electrons (e.g., Na+, Cu2+, Fe3+).\n\n**Anion** - A negatively charged ion formed when an atom gains one or more electrons (e.g., Cl-, SO4 2-, CO3 2-).\n\n**Precipitate** - An insoluble solid formed when two soluble solutions are mixed and an insoluble product is produced.\n\n**Gelatinous precipitate** - A jelly-like, soft precipitate (e.g., Al(OH)3, Fe(OH)3).\n\n**Amphoteric hydroxide** - A metal hydroxide that reacts with both acids AND alkalis. Examples: Al(OH)3, Zn(OH)2, Pb(OH)2.\n\n**Flame test** - A test in which a clean wire loop is dipped in a sample and held in a Bunsen flame; the colour of the flame indicates which metal ion is present.\n\n**Confirmatory test** - A second, more specific test done to confirm the identity of an ion already suspected from a preliminary test.\n\n**Limewater** - A solution of calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2, used to test for CO2; turns milky when CO2 is bubbled through.\n\n**Brown ring test** - A test for nitrate ions (NO3-) using iron(II) sulphate solution and concentrated sulphuric acid; a brown ring forms at the junction of the liquids.\n\n**Fehling''s solution** - A blue alkaline copper(II) solution used to test for reducing sugars; turns brick-red/orange when a reducing sugar is present.\n\n**Benedict''s solution** - Similar to Fehling''s; blue solution that gives an orange/red precipitate with reducing sugars.\n\n**Tollens'' reagent (silver mirror test)** - An ammoniacal silver nitrate solution; gives a silver mirror with aldehydes.\n\n**Iodoform test** - Reaction of iodine and NaOH with methyl ketones (CH3COR) or ethanol to give yellow iodoform (CHI3) precipitate.\n\n**Lead acetate paper** - Filter paper soaked in Pb(CH3COO)2 solution; turns black with H2S gas (forms PbS).\n\n**Starch-iodide paper** - Filter paper soaked in starch and KI solution; turns blue-black in the presence of oxidising agents (Cl2, Br2) which oxidise I- to I2.\n\n**Complex ion** - An ion formed when a central metal ion is surrounded by ligands (molecules or ions that donate electron pairs). Example: [Cu(NH3)4]2+ (deep blue tetraamminecopper(II)).\n\n**AgNO3 test** - Addition of silver nitrate solution (acidified with HNO3) to test for halide ions: Cl- (white), Br- (cream), I- (yellow) precipitates.\n\n**BaCl2 test** - Addition of barium chloride solution (acidified with HCl) to test for sulphate ions; white precipitate of BaSO4 is insoluble in excess HCl.',
     'definitions', 3),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Worked Examples: Systematic Ion Identification',
     E'## Worked Example 1: Identify an Unknown Salt\n\n**Observations on an unknown white solid X:**\n1. Dissolves in water to give a colourless solution\n2. Flame test: brick red colour\n3. Adding dilute HCl: no effervescence\n4. Adding NaOH solution: white precipitate, insoluble in excess\n5. Adding BaCl2 + dilute HCl: white precipitate that does not dissolve in excess HCl\n\n**Deduce the identity of X, giving reasoning for each test.**\n\n**Solution:**\n\n| Test | Observation | Deduction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Flame test (brick red) | Brick red | Calcium (Ca2+) is present |\n| Dilute HCl (no effervescence) | No gas | No carbonate (CO3 2-) or sulphite (SO3 2-) |\n| NaOH (white ppt, insoluble in excess) | White ppt, stays | Ca(OH)2 confirms Ca2+; not Al3+/Pb2+/Zn2+ (those dissolve in excess) |\n| BaCl2 + HCl (white ppt, insoluble) | White ppt insoluble | Sulphate (SO4 2-) is present; not sulphite (BaSO3 dissolves in HCl) |\n\n**Conclusion:** X is **calcium sulphate (CaSO4)**\n\n**Confirmatory test:** Dissolve in hot water, add AgNO3 — no precipitate (no halide); retest BaCl2 gives white precipitate insoluble in acid. Confirmed: CaSO4.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Identify Cation from NaOH and NH3 Tests\n\n**An aqueous solution of salt Y gave the following:**\n- With NaOH: blue precipitate, insoluble in excess\n- With NH3 solution: blue precipitate, dissolves in excess to give a deep blue solution\n\n**Identify the cation in Y.**\n\n**Answer:**\n- Blue precipitate with NaOH suggests Cu2+\n- Blue precipitate with dilute NH3 confirms Cu2+\n- Deep blue solution with excess NH3 is the complex ion [Cu(NH3)4]2+ (tetraamminecopper(II)) — this is diagnostic for Cu2+\n\n**Conclusion:** Cation is **Cu2+ (copper(II))**\n\nEquation with NaOH: Cu2+(aq) + 2OH-(aq) --> Cu(OH)2(s) (blue precipitate)\nEquation with excess NH3: Cu(OH)2(s) + 4NH3(aq) --> [Cu(NH3)4]2+(aq) + 2OH-(aq) (deep blue solution)\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Gas Identification\n\n**A gas is evolved when an unknown compound is heated. Describe TWO tests to identify each of the following gases, stating what you observe:**\na) CO2\nb) NH3\nc) Cl2\n\n**Answer:**\na) CO2:\n- Test 1: Bubble through limewater (Ca(OH)2 solution) — turns milky/cloudy due to CaCO3 formation\n  Ca(OH)2 + CO2 --> CaCO3 + H2O\n- Test 2: Hold a damp blue litmus paper — turns red (CO2 forms carbonic acid, slightly acidic)\n\nb) NH3:\n- Test 1: Hold moist red litmus paper near the gas — turns blue (NH3 is alkaline/basic)\n- Test 2: Hold a glass rod dipped in HCl near the gas — dense white smoke of NH4Cl forms\n  NH3(g) + HCl(g) --> NH4Cl(s) (white smoke)\n\nc) Cl2:\n- Test 1: Hold moist starch-iodide paper — turns blue-black (Cl2 oxidises I- to I2; I2 + starch --> blue colour)\n  Cl2 + 2KI --> 2KCl + I2\n- Test 2: Hold damp blue litmus paper — turns red then is bleached (Cl2 dissolves in water to form HOCl which bleaches)\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Distinguishing Two Similar Ions\n\n**Question:** You have two test tubes — one contains FeSO4 solution, the other contains Fe2(SO4)3 solution. Both give white precipitate with BaCl2. Describe ONE test to distinguish them.\n\n**Answer:**\nAdd NaOH solution to each:\n- FeSO4 (Fe2+ ions): green gelatinous precipitate of Fe(OH)2 that may turn brown at the surface\n  Fe2+(aq) + 2OH-(aq) --> Fe(OH)2(s) (green)\n- Fe2(SO4)3 (Fe3+ ions): red-brown (rust-coloured) gelatinous precipitate of Fe(OH)3\n  Fe3+(aq) + 3OH-(aq) --> Fe(OH)3(s) (red-brown)\n\nAlternatively: Add KSCN solution:\n- Fe3+ gives blood-red colouration: Fe3+ + 3SCN- --> Fe(SCN)3 (red)\n- Fe2+ gives no red colour with KSCN',
     'examples', 4),
    (v_topic_id,
     'WASSCE Practice: Qualitative Analysis',
     E'## WASSCE-Style Questions: Qualitative Analysis\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**1.** A solution gives a white precipitate with NaOH that dissolves in excess. The cation present is most likely:\nA) Fe3+\nB) Cu2+\nC) Zn2+\nD) Ca2+\n**Answer: C** — Zn2+ gives white precipitate that dissolves in excess NaOH: Zn(OH)2 + 2OH- --> [Zn(OH)4]2-. (Al3+ and Pb2+ also dissolve, but from the options C is correct.)\n\n**2.** What is the flame colour for sodium?\nA) Brick red\nB) Lilac\nC) Blue-green\nD) Persistent yellow-orange\n**Answer: D**\n\n**3.** A white precipitate forms when silver nitrate is added to a solution. It turns cream, not white. Which anion is present?\nA) Cl-\nB) Br-\nC) I-\nD) SO4 2-\n**Answer: B** — AgBr is cream coloured; AgCl is white; AgI is yellow.\n\n**4.** Which test best confirms the presence of CO3 2- (carbonate)?\nA) Add HNO3 — white precipitate forms\nB) Add HCl — gas produced that turns limewater milky\nC) Add NaOH — blue precipitate forms\nD) Add BaCl2 — cream precipitate forms\n**Answer: B**\n\n**5.** A gas turns moist red litmus paper blue and produces white fumes with HCl. The gas is:\nA) Hydrogen\nB) Chlorine\nC) Ammonia\nD) Sulphur dioxide\n**Answer: C**\n\n---\n\n### Section B: Structured Questions\n\n**Question 1 (WASSCE 2019 style):**\nAn unknown white solid W was analysed as follows:\n- It dissolved completely in water to give a pale green solution\n- Adding NaOH solution gave a green precipitate that did not dissolve in excess\n- The green precipitate turned red-brown on standing in air\n- Adding BaCl2 solution (acidified with HCl) gave a white precipitate insoluble in excess HCl\n- Flame test: no distinctive colour\n\na) Identify the cation in W. Give the ionic equation for its reaction with NaOH. [3 marks]\nb) Identify the anion in W. Give the ionic equation for its reaction with BaCl2. [2 marks]\nc) Name the compound W. [1 mark]\nd) Write the equation for the conversion of the green precipitate to the red-brown product. [2 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) Fe2+ (iron(II)) [1]; green precipitate of iron(II) hydroxide [1]\nFe2+(aq) + 2OH-(aq) --> Fe(OH)2(s) (green precipitate) [1]\n\nb) SO4 2- (sulphate) [1]\nBa2+(aq) + SO4 2-(aq) --> BaSO4(s) (white precipitate, insoluble in HCl) [1]\n\nc) Iron(II) sulphate (FeSO4) / ferrous sulphate [1]\n\nd) The green Fe(OH)2 is oxidised by atmospheric oxygen to red-brown Fe(OH)3:\n4Fe(OH)2(s) + O2(g) + 2H2O(l) --> 4Fe(OH)3(s) [2]\n\n**Question 2:**\na) Describe how you would test a colourless gas to show it is ammonia. Give TWO independent tests with observations. [4 marks]\nb) An unknown solution Q gives a yellow precipitate when silver nitrate solution is added. What is the anion in Q? Give the ionic equation for the reaction. [2 marks]\nc) A solution R gives a deep blue colour with excess ammonia. Identify the ion responsible and name the complex formed. [2 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na) Test 1: Hold moist red litmus paper near the gas — the litmus paper turns blue, confirming the gas is alkaline/basic [2]\nTest 2: Hold glass rod dipped in concentrated HCl near the gas — dense white fumes of ammonium chloride form [2]\n\nb) Iodide (I-) [1]\nAg+(aq) + I-(aq) --> AgI(s) (yellow precipitate) [1]\n\nc) Cu2+ (copper(II)) [1]; tetraamminecopper(II) ion: [Cu(NH3)4]2+ [1]',
     'practice', 5);
  END IF;

  /* ============================================================
     TOPIC 20: NUCLEAR CHEMISTRY AND RADIOACTIVITY
     ============================================================ */
  SELECT id INTO v_topic_id FROM topics WHERE name ILIKE '%nuclear%' OR name ILIKE '%radioactiv%' LIMIT 1;
  IF v_topic_id IS NOT NULL THEN
    DELETE FROM topic_sections WHERE topic_id = v_topic_id;
    INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
    (v_topic_id,
     'Radioactivity: Types, Properties and Detection',
     E'## Nuclear Chemistry and Radioactivity\n\nRadioactivity is the spontaneous emission of radiation from an unstable atomic nucleus. It is a nuclear phenomenon — chemical reactions do NOT affect the rate of radioactive decay.\n\n### Why Are Nuclei Radioactive?\n- Nuclei are held together by the **strong nuclear force**\n- If the neutron-to-proton (n/p) ratio is too high or too low, the nucleus is unstable\n- Unstable nuclei emit radiation to move towards a more stable configuration\n- All elements with Z > 83 (bismuth) are radioactive\n\n### The Three Types of Radiation\n\n| Property | Alpha (alpha) | Beta (beta) | Gamma (gamma) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Symbol | 4/2 He (helium nucleus) | 0/-1 e (electron) | 0/0 gamma (photon) |\n| Composition | 2 protons + 2 neutrons | High-speed electron | High-energy electromagnetic radiation |\n| Charge | +2 | -1 | 0 |\n| Mass | 4 u | ~0 | 0 |\n| Speed | ~5% speed of light | up to 90% speed of light | speed of light |\n| Ionising power | Very high (strongest ioniser) | Medium | Low |\n| Penetrating power | Low (stopped by paper or few cm air) | Medium (stopped by 3mm Al) | High (reduced by thick lead or concrete) |\n| Deflection in E/B field | Deflected (positive) | Deflected (negative, more) | Not deflected |\n\n### Detection of Radiation\n\n**Geiger-Muller (GM) tube:**\n- Radiation ionises argon gas inside the tube\n- Each ionisation event causes a pulse of electric current\n- Connected to counter or rate meter\n- Can detect all three types (with appropriate window thickness)\n\n**Cloud chamber:**\n- Supercooled alcohol vapour condenses on ions formed by radiation\n- Visible tracks: alpha = thick, straight tracks; beta = thin, curved tracks; gamma = faint, scattered\n\n**Photographic film badge:**\n- Radiation fogs photographic film\n- Used by radiation workers (radiographers, nuclear power workers) to monitor dose\n\n**Scintillation counter:**\n- Radiation causes flashes of light in a sodium iodide crystal\n- Very sensitive; used in medical imaging\n\n### Background Radiation\nBackground radiation is low-level radiation always present from natural and artificial sources:\n\n**Natural sources (~85%):**\n- Radon gas (from granite rocks) — largest contributor\n- Cosmic rays from space\n- Radioactive isotopes in food (K-40, C-14)\n- Rocks and soil (uranium, thorium, radium)\n\n**Artificial sources (~15%):**\n- Medical X-rays and nuclear medicine\n- Nuclear weapons testing fallout\n- Nuclear power stations (small contribution)\n\n**West African relevance:**\n- Granite-rich regions (e.g., parts of Ghana''s Eastern and Ashanti regions) have higher radon levels\n- Ghana has a nuclear research reactor at BNARI (Biotechnology and Nuclear Agriculture Research Institute)',
     'overview', 1),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Nuclear Equations, Half-Life and Nuclear Reactions',
     E'## Nuclear Equations\n\n### Rules for Writing Nuclear Equations\n1. The sum of mass numbers on each side must be equal\n2. The sum of atomic numbers on each side must be equal\n3. Radiation is shown as a particle with its mass and atomic number\n\n### Alpha Decay (alpha emission)\n- Mass number decreases by 4\n- Atomic number decreases by 2\n- The nucleus becomes a different element\n\n**Example:** Radium-226 decays by alpha emission:\n226/88 Ra --> 222/86 Rn + 4/2 He\n\nCheck: Mass: 226 = 222 + 4 ✓  |  Atomic number: 88 = 86 + 2 ✓\n\n### Beta Decay (beta emission)\n- Mass number unchanged\n- Atomic number increases by 1 (a neutron converts to a proton + electron)\n- Neutron --> Proton + Beta particle: 1/0 n --> 1/1 p + 0/-1 e\n\n**Example:** Carbon-14 (radiocarbon dating):\n14/6 C --> 14/7 N + 0/-1 e\n\nCheck: Mass: 14 = 14 + 0 ✓  |  Atomic number: 6 = 7 + (-1) ✓\n\n### Gamma Emission\n- Often accompanies alpha or beta decay\n- No change in mass number or atomic number\n- Releases excess energy from the nucleus\n\n### Half-Life\n**Definition:** The time taken for HALF the nuclei in a radioactive sample to decay (or for the activity to fall to half its initial value).\n\n**Key features of half-life:**\n- Constant for a given isotope (not affected by temperature, pressure, chemical state)\n- Ranges from milliseconds to billions of years\n- After n half-lives, fraction remaining = (1/2)^n\n\n**Half-life formula:**\nN(t) = N0 x (1/2)^(t/T1/2)\n\nWhere:\n- N0 = initial number of atoms (or activity)\n- N(t) = number remaining at time t\n- T1/2 = half-life\n\n### Important Radioactive Isotopes\n\n| Isotope | Half-life | Use / Significance |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carbon-14 (C-14) | 5730 years | Radiocarbon dating of archaeological/biological specimens |\n| Uranium-235 | 703 million years | Nuclear fuel; nuclear weapons |\n| Iodine-131 | 8 days | Diagnosis and treatment of thyroid cancer |\n| Technetium-99m | 6 hours | Medical imaging (gamma camera scans) |\n| Cobalt-60 | 5.3 years | Radiotherapy (treating cancer); food irradiation |\n| Plutonium-239 | 24,000 years | Nuclear weapons; long-term radioactive waste concern |\n| Radon-222 | 3.8 days | Natural indoor air pollutant (from granite) |\n\n### Nuclear Fission and Fusion\n\n**Nuclear Fission:**\n- A heavy nucleus is split into two smaller nuclei when bombarded by a neutron\n- Releases huge amounts of energy (E = mc2)\n- Chain reaction: each fission releases 2-3 neutrons, which cause more fissions\n- Example: U-235 fission:\n  235/92 U + 1/0 n --> 141/56 Ba + 92/36 Kr + 3(1/0 n) + energy\n- Controlled fission: nuclear power stations\n- Uncontrolled fission: atomic bomb\n\n**Nuclear Fusion:**\n- Two light nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus\n- Requires extremely high temperature (10 million degrees C) to overcome electrostatic repulsion\n- Releases even more energy per kg than fission\n- Example: hydrogen fusion in the Sun:\n  2/1 H + 3/1 H --> 4/2 He + 1/0 n + energy\n- Used in hydrogen bomb (thermonuclear weapon)\n- Controlled fusion: the future of clean energy (ITER project)\n\n### Radiocarbon Dating\n1. C-14 is continuously produced in the upper atmosphere: 14/7 N + 1/0 n --> 14/6 C + 1/1 p\n2. Living organisms take in C-14 with food; C-14/C-12 ratio stays constant\n3. When organism dies, no more C-14 intake; C-14 decays (T1/2 = 5730 years)\n4. Measure C-14/C-12 ratio to calculate age of sample\n5. Useful for: artefacts, charcoal, bones (up to ~50,000 years)\n6. West African examples: dating of Nok culture terracottas (Nigeria, ~2500 years old); ancient iron smelting sites in West Africa',
     'explanation', 2),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Key Definitions: Nuclear Chemistry and Radioactivity',
     E'## Essential Nuclear Chemistry Definitions\n\n**Radioactivity** - The spontaneous emission of particles or electromagnetic radiation from the unstable nucleus of an atom.\n\n**Radioisotope (radioactive isotope)** - An isotope with an unstable nucleus that spontaneously decays, emitting radiation.\n\n**Alpha particle (alpha radiation)** - A particle consisting of 2 protons and 2 neutrons (a helium-4 nucleus), emitted by the nucleus during alpha decay. Symbol: 4/2 He.\n\n**Beta particle (beta radiation)** - A high-energy electron emitted from the nucleus when a neutron is converted to a proton. Symbol: 0/-1 e.\n\n**Gamma radiation** - High-energy electromagnetic radiation emitted from an excited nucleus following alpha or beta decay. No mass or charge.\n\n**Background radiation** - The low-level ionising radiation always present in the environment from natural sources (radon, cosmic rays, food) and artificial sources (medical X-rays, nuclear testing).\n\n**Half-life (T1/2)** - The time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a given sample to decay; or the time for the activity of a sample to fall to half its original value.\n\n**Activity** - The number of nuclear disintegrations per second in a radioactive sample. Measured in Becquerels (Bq). 1 Bq = 1 decay per second.\n\n**Decay constant (lambda)** - The probability of a given nucleus decaying per unit time. Related to half-life by: T1/2 = ln2 / lambda = 0.693 / lambda.\n\n**Nuclear fission** - The splitting of a heavy atomic nucleus (e.g., U-235) into two smaller nuclei with the release of a large amount of energy and several neutrons.\n\n**Nuclear fusion** - The combining of two light nuclei (e.g., hydrogen isotopes) to form a heavier nucleus with the release of a large amount of energy.\n\n**Chain reaction** - A self-sustaining sequence of nuclear fission reactions in which neutrons released by one fission cause further fissions. Controlled in reactors; uncontrolled in bombs.\n\n**Critical mass** - The minimum mass of fissile material required to sustain a self-sustaining chain reaction.\n\n**Moderator** - A material (e.g., water, graphite) in a nuclear reactor that slows down fast neutrons to thermal (slow) neutrons, which are more effective at causing U-235 fission.\n\n**Control rods** - Rods made of neutron-absorbing material (boron or cadmium) inserted into a nuclear reactor to absorb excess neutrons and control the reaction rate.\n\n**Radiocarbon dating** - Using the known half-life of C-14 (5730 years) to determine the age of organic materials by measuring the ratio of C-14 to C-12.\n\n**Ionising radiation** - Radiation with enough energy to knock electrons from atoms, forming ions. All three types (alpha, beta, gamma) are ionising; gamma is least ionising per unit path length.\n\n**Absorbed dose** - The energy of radiation absorbed per unit mass of tissue. Measured in Gray (Gy). 1 Gy = 1 J/kg.\n\n**Dose equivalent (Sievert, Sv)** - A measure of the biological damage caused by radiation, accounting for the type of radiation (alpha is 20x more damaging than gamma per unit energy absorbed).\n\n**Nuclear waste** - Radioactive materials produced by nuclear power generation and nuclear weapons, classified as low-, intermediate-, or high-level waste depending on activity.',
     'definitions', 3),
    (v_topic_id,
     'Worked Examples: Nuclear Equations and Half-Life Calculations',
     E'## Worked Example 1: Completing Nuclear Equations\n\n**Question:** Complete these nuclear equations:\n\na) 238/92 U --> ? + 4/2 He\nb) 32/15 P --> 32/? S + 0/-1 e\nc) ? + 4/2 He --> 30/15 P + 1/0 n\n\n**Solution:**\n\na) Mass number: 238 = ? + 4, so ? = 234\n   Atomic number: 92 = ? + 2, so ? = 90\n   Element with Z = 90 is Thorium (Th)\n   Answer: 238/92 U --> 234/90 Th + 4/2 He\n\nb) Mass number: 32 = 32 + 0, so mass of S = 32 ✓\n   Atomic number: 15 = ? + (-1), so ? = 16\n   Element with Z = 16 is Sulphur (S)\n   Answer: 32/15 P --> 32/16 S + 0/-1 e\n\nc) Mass number: ? + 4 = 30 + 1, so ? = 27\n   Atomic number: ? + 2 = 15 + 0, so ? = 13\n   Element with Z = 13, mass = 27 is Aluminium-27\n   Answer: 27/13 Al + 4/2 He --> 30/15 P + 1/0 n\n   (This is the reaction used by Irene and Frederic Joliot-Curie to produce the first artificial radioisotope)\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Half-Life Calculation\n\n**Question:** A sample of iodine-131 has an initial activity of 3200 Bq. Its half-life is 8 days. Calculate:\na) The activity after 24 days\nb) The time for the activity to fall to 100 Bq\n\n**Solution:**\n\na) Number of half-lives in 24 days = 24/8 = 3 half-lives\n\nAfter 1 half-life (8 days): 3200/2 = 1600 Bq\nAfter 2 half-lives (16 days): 1600/2 = 800 Bq\nAfter 3 half-lives (24 days): 800/2 = 400 Bq\n\nAlternatively: Activity = 3200 x (1/2)^3 = 3200 x (1/8) = 400 Bq\n\nb) We need to find t such that 3200 x (1/2)^(t/8) = 100\n\n(1/2)^(t/8) = 100/3200 = 1/32\n\nNote: 1/32 = (1/2)^5\n\nSo t/8 = 5, therefore t = 40 days\n\nAlternative counting:\n3200 --> 1600 (8d) --> 800 (16d) --> 400 (24d) --> 200 (32d) --> 100 (40d)\n\n**Answer: a) 400 Bq    b) 40 days**\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 3: Radiocarbon Dating\n\n**Question:** An archaeologist finds charcoal from a fire pit at an ancient settlement site in northern Ghana. The C-14 activity is measured to be 25% of what would be expected in living wood. The half-life of C-14 is 5730 years. How old is the site?\n\n**Solution:**\n\nActivity has fallen to 25% = (1/4) of the original.\n\n(1/2)^n = 1/4 where n = number of half-lives\n\n(1/2)^n = (1/2)^2, so n = 2 half-lives\n\nAge = 2 x 5730 = 11,460 years\n\n**The site is approximately 11,460 years old.**\n\nThis places it at the end of the last ice age, consistent with early human settlement in the West African savanna as the climate became wetter.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 4: Uses and Dangers of Radioactivity\n\n**Question:** Explain why:\na) Alpha sources are used in smoke detectors but NOT for medical imaging\nb) Gamma sources are used for cancer radiotherapy\nc) Iodine-131 (T1/2 = 8 days) is preferred over Iodine-129 (T1/2 = 15.7 million years) for thyroid treatment\n\n**Answer:**\n\na) Alpha particles are the most ionising radiation. In smoke detectors, alpha particles ionise air between two plates, allowing a small current to flow; smoke interrupts this current, triggering the alarm [good short-range ionisation is useful here]. Alpha cannot be used for medical imaging because alpha particles are stopped by a few centimetres of air or skin — they would not penetrate the body to reach detectors outside. They would also deliver a high radiation dose to tissue if injected.\n\nb) Gamma radiation is the most penetrating — it can pass through the body and be focused on a tumour from outside. The tumour is targeted from multiple angles so the tumour receives a high dose while surrounding tissue receives a lower dose. Gamma radiation damages DNA in cancer cells, preventing them from dividing. Alpha and beta are too poorly penetrating (alpha) or too dangerous to tissues along the entry path (beta) for external beam therapy.\n\nc) Iodine-131 (T1/2 = 8 days) is preferred because:\n- Short enough to reduce the period the patient is radioactive (reduced long-term exposure to others)\n- Long enough to allow effective treatment of thyroid cancer before most of the activity has decayed\n- After ~10-12 half-lives (~80-96 days), the patient''s activity is negligible\n\nIodine-129''s 15.7 million year half-life means it would remain in the patient''s body for their entire lifetime, delivering continuous radiation — unacceptably dangerous.',
     'examples', 4),
    (v_topic_id,
     'WASSCE Practice: Nuclear Chemistry and Radioactivity',
     E'## WASSCE-Style Questions: Nuclear Chemistry\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**1.** Which type of radiation is NOT deflected by electric and magnetic fields?\nA) Alpha\nB) Beta\nC) Gamma\nD) Both alpha and beta\n**Answer: C** — Gamma radiation carries no charge, so it is not deflected by electric or magnetic fields.\n\n**2.** After 3 half-lives, the fraction of a radioactive sample that has decayed is:\nA) 1/8\nB) 3/8\nC) 7/8\nD) 1/4\n**Answer: C** — After 3 half-lives, 1/8 remains, so 7/8 has decayed.\n\n**3.** In alpha decay, the mass number of the daughter nucleus compared to the parent is:\nA) Increased by 2\nB) Decreased by 4\nC) Decreased by 2\nD) Unchanged\n**Answer: B** — Alpha particle (mass 4) is emitted; daughter mass = parent mass - 4.\n\n**4.** Carbon-14 dating can be used to determine the age of:\nA) Iron tools\nB) Gold coins\nC) Ancient wood or charcoal\nD) Limestone rocks\n**Answer: C** — C-14 dating works only on once-living organic material that was exchanging carbon with the atmosphere.\n\n**5.** Which radiation has the GREATEST ionising power?\nA) Gamma\nB) Beta\nC) Alpha\nD) X-rays\n**Answer: C** — Alpha particles are the most massive and most highly charged; they ionise densely but are quickly stopped.\n\n---\n\n### Section B: Structured Questions\n\n**Question 1:**\na) Complete the following nuclear equations:\n   i) 226/88 Ra --> ? + 4/2 He [2 marks]\n   ii) 14/6 C --> 14/7 N + ? [1 mark]\nb) Define the term ''half-life'' of a radioactive element. [2 marks]\nc) A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 20 minutes. A sample has an initial activity of 1600 disintegrations per second. Calculate the activity after 1 hour. [3 marks]\nd) State ONE safety precaution that should be taken when handling radioactive materials in a laboratory. [1 mark]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na i) Mass: 226 = ? + 4, Z: 88 = ? + 2, so daughter is 222/86 Rn [2]\n   226/88 Ra --> 222/86 Rn + 4/2 He\n\na ii) 0/-1 e (beta particle) [1]\n\nb) The half-life is the time taken for half the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay [1]; it is constant for a given isotope and unaffected by physical or chemical conditions [1]\n\nc) 1 hour = 60 minutes; number of half-lives = 60/20 = 3\nActivity = 1600 x (1/2)^3 = 1600 x (1/8) = 200 disintegrations per second [3]\n\nd) Any one: Handle with long tongs (do not touch directly); work behind lead/concrete shielding; wear dosimeter badge to monitor exposure; work in a well-ventilated area; minimise time of exposure; store sources in lead-lined containers [1]\n\n**Question 2:**\na) State TWO differences between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion. [4 marks]\nb) Explain how a nuclear reactor uses the fission of U-235 to generate electricity. Your answer should include the role of: fuel rods, moderator, control rods, and coolant. [6 marks]\nc) State TWO advantages of nuclear power over coal-fired power stations for electricity generation. [2 marks]\nd) Suggest why radioactive waste from nuclear reactors is a major problem. [2 marks]\n\n**Model Answer:**\na)\n- Fission involves splitting of a heavy nucleus; fusion involves combining of two light nuclei [2]\n- Fission occurs at room temperature (needs only slow neutrons); fusion requires extremely high temperature (millions of degrees) to overcome electrostatic repulsion of nuclei [2]\n(Accept: fission uses U-235/Pu-239; fusion uses hydrogen isotopes H-2 and H-3)\n\nb)\n- Fuel rods (uranium oxide, UO2): contain U-235 which undergoes fission when struck by slow neutrons, releasing energy and 2-3 fast neutrons [1]\n- Moderator (water or graphite): slows the fast neutrons produced by fission to thermal/slow neutrons; slow neutrons are more effective at causing further fission of U-235 [1]\n- Control rods (boron or cadmium): absorb excess neutrons to prevent the reaction rate from increasing; pushed in to slow the reaction, pulled out to speed it up; allow safe control of the chain reaction [2]\n- Coolant (water under pressure): flows past the fuel rods, removing heat produced by fission [1]; this heat converts water in a secondary circuit to steam, which drives turbines connected to generators to produce electricity [1]\n\nc) Any two: Nuclear power produces NO carbon dioxide or greenhouse gases during operation (lower climate impact); nuclear fuel (U-235) is very energy-dense — a small amount produces enormous energy; no sulphur dioxide or nitrogen oxide emissions (no acid rain) [1 each]\n\nd) Radioactive waste contains long-lived isotopes (e.g., Pu-239, T1/2 = 24,000 years) that remain dangerously radioactive for thousands of years [1]; must be stored safely in engineered containment (deep geological repositories) with no possibility of leakage into groundwater or ecosystem for time periods longer than human civilisation has existed [1]',
     'practice', 5);
